<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Olga of Kiev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 890–<a href="/years/969">969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Saint Olga · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Olga was the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igor-svyatoslavich">Prince Igor</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> for her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav</a> after Igor's death in 945. She took harsh revenge on the Drevlians for her husband's killing and reorganised the collection of tribute. Baptised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in the 950s, she was the first ruler of Rus' to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and is venerated as a saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olga-of-kiev.jpg" alt="Olga of Kiev" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mikhail Nesterov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Olga_by_Nesterov_in_1892_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Oleg and his successors Igor, <b>Olga</b> and Sviatoslav I extended tribute over East Slavic tribes such as the Polianians, Drevlians, Severians and Krivichians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Envoys of the pagan ruler of the Kievan Rus’ reported that inside it “we did not know where we were, in heaven or on earth”, and <b>Olga of Kiev</b> was probably baptised in its baptistery.</blockquote><a href="/work/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Igor's widow <b>Olga</b> took vengeance in 946, burying and burning Drevlian envoys and setting Iskorosten alight with birds carrying burning tinder.</blockquote><a href="/state/drevlians">Drevlians</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=57.82,28.33&amp;year=941" aria-label="Pskov on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5156 209 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Olga of Kiev"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5606 479h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5606 479h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=57.82,28.33&amp;year=941">What was here in 941 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
